window.docContents[129].resolve({"/Lecture-1___-Motivation-and-Propositional-Logic/Worked-Examples/#Formal-Software-Verification--Lecture-1___-Motivation-and-Propositional-Logic--Worked-Examples--Modus-Ponens":{"contents":"An implication and its antecedent, both projected from the conjunction, combine by →E to give the consequent.Modus ponens is Latin, short for modus ponendo ponens, \"the mode that affirms by affirming\".\n\n   [(P→Q)∧P]           [(P→Q)∧P]\n  ───────────── ∧E₁    ───────────── ∧E₂\n      P → Q                  P\n    ────────────────────────────── →E\n                 Q\n   ──────────────────────────────── →I\n        (P → Q) ∧ P → Q\n\n\nexample (P Q : Prop) : (P → Q) ∧ P → Q :=\n  fun h => h.left h.right\n\n\nexample (P Q : Prop) : (P → Q) ∧ P → Q := by\n  intro h\n  apply h.left\n  exact h.right\n\n\n","context":"Formal Software Verification\u0009Lecture 1: Motivation and Propositional Logic\u0009Worked Examples","header":"1.8.3. Modus Ponens","id":"/Lecture-1___-Motivation-and-Propositional-Logic/Worked-Examples/#Formal-Software-Verification--Lecture-1___-Motivation-and-Propositional-Logic--Worked-Examples--Modus-Ponens"}});